### Section 5.4: the node-wise decoupled filter, the variant that lifts the
### quadratic covariance limit at the cost of more epochs per cost decade.
### Compare with input_nn.dat, which is the same case with the dense filter.
###
### PINN demo trained by the extended Kalman filter instead of a
### gradient method.  Same problem, same data and same network as
### zwork/pinn_poisson2d, so the two directories are a direct comparison:
###
###   Adam    20000 epochs -> cost 1.1e-2, rms error 3.1e-3 against u*
###   Kalman    300 epochs -> cost 4.8e-9, rms error 1.8e-5 against u*
###
### The filter presents one scalar observable at a time.  Here the
### observable of a collocation point is the residual and its target is
### zero, and the Jacobian row dR/dw comes from the same seed the
### gradient path uses.  The covariance is n_w x n_w, so this method
### belongs to small networks; the run reports its size on startup.
###
### GD_param for KALMAN:
###   p1 = initial diagonal of the covariance P
###   p2 = initial forgetting factor lambda
###   p3 = lambda0 of the schedule lambda <- lambda0*lambda + 1 - lambda0
Output_deriv 1 /
Init_w       Glorot_norm
Rand_seed    20260730 / fixed seed (reproducibility)
Restart      0 /
Nlayer       4 / L
2 / ndim(1): (x,y)
20 /
20 /
1 / ndim(L)
Loss_term  DATA         160  data.dat     1.d0  / boundary condition
Loss_term  COLLOCATION  400  colloc.dat   1.d-3 / interior residual
Residual 3 /
LIN   1.d0    2 0 /  u_xx
LIN   1.d0    0 2 /  u_yy
SRC  -1.d0        /  minus the source column of colloc.dat
Exact_solution 1 /
Hod_check 1 /
Num_validation 30 /
Validation_cyc 20 /
P_max          500 /
Epoch      3000 /
Activation TANH /
Conv       1.d-12 /
Num_batch  40 /
Checkpoint_cyc 100 /
Hod_K      2 /
1.d0
0.d0
0.d0
Hod_dense  1 /
GD_method  KALMAN
GD_param   1.d-3 0.98d0 0.9995d0 0.d0 0.d0 /   p0 lowered: the block-diagonal covariance loses positive definiteness at 1.d-2
Kalman_mode DECOUPLED /
